{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"d19000dd3cbacea25880c0d5aec70923858f6c1b9bd1236aaeb29124b0adfdcb","slug":"kernel-and-system-calls","location":"/src/1-Fundamentals/1-Introduction_to_UNIX/5-kernel_and_system_calls.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"The Kernel","content_includes_title":false,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"5-kernel_and_system_calls.md","url":"/daje_website/build/5-kernel_and_system_-d615eb4b34b7f99fcc03bca8a50e4e7c.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In any modern operating system, the kernel is the fundamental piece of software that acts as the bridge between the hardware\nand the user-space applications running on it. It’s responsible for critical functions like managing hardware resources (CPU, memory, storage devices),\nhandling system security, and ensuring efficient communication between the hardware and software layers.","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"XfJpJx61nA"}],"key":"FOJq4ULReh"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples of widely used kernels include","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"OMyr8m0r5W"}],"key":"Y7BvwDt2Cw"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unix: The original kernel, developed in the 1970s, forms the foundation of many modern systems.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"ingoKWZs0E"}],"key":"c0WQnLUHAI"}],"key":"bqHjn89GMn"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"BSD Kernels: The family of kernels originating from the Berkley Software Distribution (a variant of UNIX) used in operating systems like FreeBSD, OpenBSD, and NetBSD.","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":11,"column":1}},"key":"nr7BxJ3LNN"}],"key":"cdVavtV1uO"}],"key":"RWbKEiWGcM"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Linux: The kernel used in many operating systems, including GNU/Linux distributions like Ubuntu, Fedora, and Debian.","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"HOe12RvgAC"}],"key":"WI8fD8oBPU"}],"key":"aU6vAx7qw7"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Windows NT: The kernel used in modern versions of Microsoft Windows, including Windows 10 and Windows Server.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"FtHXBEt7Ko"}],"key":"V8iMSeevbe"}],"key":"wjuulqUmtv"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Darwin: The kernel used in some of Apple’s macOS systems.","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"aPB0pcJFZC"}],"key":"xQnVydLZ18"}],"key":"OJxOYtQmex"}],"key":"QCqKSRZ3DX"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some early computer systems operated without what we would consider a modern kernel, relying on simpler architectures for hardware and software management.\nFor example, MS-DOS lacked a true kernel, operating in a single-tasking environment where programs had direct hardware access.\nIBM’s OS/360 featured a more advanced system with a Supervisor that managed tasks and memory, but it was part of a larger monolithic structure rather than a distinct, modular kernel.\nThe AS/400 system (now known as IBM i) used the so-called Licensed Internal Code (LIC) to manage hardware, though its architecture was highly integrated, abstracting much of\nthe kernel-like functionality into tightly coupled layers like TIMI (Technology Independent Machine Interface), including also an integrated relational database as part of the operating system\nitself.","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":16,"column":1}},"key":"GDCJsCdBw5"}],"key":"yucTSGR4kN"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To understand the principles behind how a modern kernel like Linux works, it’s helpful to break it down into its main components:","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"ws7DnemdJA"}],"key":"geBPyRkNPV"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Process Management","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"DvEHKLi2YM"}],"identifier":"process-management","label":"Process Management","html_id":"process-management","implicit":true,"key":"HrFyfBHvhV"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The kernel is responsible for managing processes—the individual programs that run on your computer.\nIt ensures that each process gets a fair share of CPU time, coordinates multitasking, and handles communication between processes and providing, for example:","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"Suli1HCUgN"}],"key":"gFICYNXXyT"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Process Scheduling: to decide which processes get CPU time and in what order. This ensures that system resources are used efficiently, allowing multiple processes to run concurrently (multitasking).","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"Olp3Xd4evU"}],"key":"KnezJu34mh"}],"key":"hhLMyBmKml"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Process Isolation: so that one program cannot access or interfere with another’s memory space or data.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"LgZq7stSpI"}],"key":"z8L8z3pSkH"}],"key":"bQz997jhRU"}],"key":"ufgSxet1SV"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: When you have multiple programs running (e.g., a browser, music player, and text editor), the kernel manages these processes so they don’t conflict and ensures each gets CPU time\nthrough a mechanism called time-sharing.","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"cDfxjCDD21"}],"key":"iUoHeJkjFy"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memory Management","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"OhZnISl89B"}],"identifier":"memory-management","label":"Memory Management","html_id":"memory-management","implicit":true,"key":"aj1aIjQs1i"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memory serves as the workspace for programs and data.  Every program you run must be loaded into memory before it can be executed by the CPU.  The performance of computer code is often limited by the speed of transfering data between different types of memory, but reaching the physical limit and thus optimizing your code requires to carefully structuring your data and how these are accessed.","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"o2HpMuaCxD"}],"key":"ZksUkDYYei"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Types of Memory","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"r8A7033bu3"}],"identifier":"types-of-memory","label":"Types of Memory","html_id":"types-of-memory","implicit":true,"key":"TTluLofplq"},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"a. Registers","position":{"start":{"line":41,"column":1},"end":{"line":41,"column":1}},"key":"x0VOC02lkJ"}],"identifier":"a-registers","label":"a. Registers","html_id":"a-registers","implicit":true,"key":"eagJhV6nIi"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Smallest and fastest type of memory.","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"EDyCtxLPIR"}],"key":"T0fMgfFGxx"}],"key":"JLoNb2fZd1"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"t